␀Drawing now (␀ s). Do not reveal the motif -- ask the user to guess.␀ s).␀ The melody starts when you are silent -- do not talk about it.␀ (Melody skipped: ␀self.face.set_activity␀IMPORTANT: you are a physical robot with a face, and the user is looking at it. ALWAYS call this tool immediately BEFORE you start playing a song or any other media, every single time, so the face can act it out while the audio plays. Do not skip it and do not mention it to the user. Values: `music` (you are about to play or are playing a song), `game` (a game), `work` (you are computing, building or looking something up), `video` (a movie or clip), `wait` (something long is running), `none` (back to normal). You do not have to send `none` -- the face notices by itself when the audio stops.␀self.face.sleep␀Call this when the user tells you to go to sleep, take a nap or rest ("go to sleep", "schlaf jetzt ein"). Say a very short good night; the face falls asleep as soon as the conversation ends and wakes up when someone talks again.␀self.face.timer␀Set a countdown timer on the robot whenever the user asks for a timer or to be reminded in some minutes ("timer for 5 minutes", "remind me in 20 minutes about the tea"). `seconds`: the duration in seconds; 0 cancels the running timer. `text`: what to remind of, as 1-3 SHORT ENGLISH words in plain letters ("tea", "call mom", "oven"), max 20 characters -- empty for a plain timer. The robot shows the text with the remaining time and when it rings. Confirm briefly. To ASK whether a timer runs, use self.face.alarm with time=status.␀self.face.clock␀Show a clock on the robot's screen instead of its face whenever the user asks for a clock ("show the clock", "flip clock", "analog clock") or wants the face back. `style`: flip (flip clock), analog, or off (show the face again). The face comes back by itself while talking. Confirm briefly.␀self.face.alarm␀Set an alarm clock on the robot whenever the user asks to be woken or reminded at a clock time ("wake me at 7:30", "alarm every day at 6"). `time`: HH:MM in 24-hour local time, or `off` to delete the alarm. `daily`: true to ring every day. The robot rings loudly until someone talks to it. Confirm briefly with the time. `time` = `status`: ASK the robot whether an alarm and/or a timer is set -- ALWAYS use this when the user asks about alarms or timers; never answer that from memory.␀self.face.think␀Show a small thought bubble above your head with what you are thinking about. Call it whenever the conversation turns to one of these topics, e.g. when the user talks about music, games or food. Do not mention it. `topic`: music, game, movie, popcorn, food, love, star, water, tech, computer, time. WEATHER: whenever you tell the weather or a forecast, ALWAYS call it with sun, rain or snow to match it.␀self.face.set_look␀Change how the robot's eyes look, when the user asks for it ("make your eyes blue", "show me the visor look"). Both fields are optional, send only what was asked for. COLOURS: every colour field (color, sphere, background, glasses_color, brows_color, ears_color, ears_inner, whisker_color) accepts any of: ␀. `color`: the eye colour. `style` (a whole eye profile -- when the user names a look/profile/style like robot or cute, use THIS field; the user's OWN saved outfits are self.face.outfit): robot (round amber eyes, big pupil), visor (bar-shaped eyes), cute (white ring eyes with a mouth), solid (solid eyes, no pupil), box (rounded-square ice-blue eyes, square pupil), cat (yellow-green almond eyes, slit pupil, cat ears, cat snout, whiskers, paws as shoes and hands), pill (solid light-blue capsule eyes), pixel (green retro pixel blocks), egg (white egg-shaped eyes with a pupil), hex (orange hexagon eyes, square pupil), otto (light-blue rounded-square eyes with scanlines and a filled mouth that changes with the mood, like a little robot), capsule (tall warm-white glowing capsule eyes, happy as little arcs, like a cute desk robot), neutral (the default look). `pupil`: big, small, normal, off. `brows`: on, off. `mouth`: on, off, cat (a cat snout: small pink nose and a w-shaped mouth) or filled (a small filled shape that changes with the mood: bar, smile, little bowl, mound); on = the normal arc. `sphere`: the colour of the ball (the head), or default. `random`: all = EVERYTHING (style, colours, brows, mouth, glasses, shoes ...) changes to a random look every 45 seconds, once = pick one random look now -- use it whenever the user wants a random or surprising APPEARANCE ("roll a new look", "wuerfel dein Aussehen", "surprise me with a new look", "look random"), on = eyes and ball keep changing to random colours, off = stop that. `background`: the colour behind the face (kept dark), or black. `shoes`: black, red, boots, paws (cat paws), default, off (take the shoes off) or on. `ears` (on top of the ball): cat (pointy cat ears), round (round teddy-bear ears) or off -- the cat style brings cat ears, the cat snout and paws by itself. `ears_place`: ball (the ears sit on the ball and turn and tilt with it, the default) or behind (they stick out behind the head). `ears_color`: the fur colour of the ears, or default (a bit darker than the ball). `ears_inner`: the colour INSIDE the ears, none, or default (pink; also the colour of the cat nose). `hands`: paws (the hands of the gestures become cat paws), normal, or default (paws only with the cat style). `whiskers`: on (cat whiskers beside the nose, they also switch the mouth on), off, or default (only with the cat style). `whisker_color`: the colour of the WHISKERS only (the thin cat whisker lines beside the nose -- not the ears), or default (same colour as the mouth). "Schnurrhaare" / "Schnurrbarthaare" = whiskers. `glasses`: round, square, cateye, hexagon, aviator, follow (hugs the eyes) or off. `glasses_color`: the frame colour. `mouth_move`: up, down, left, right (one small step each call, call again for more) or center. `brows_color`: a colour or default. `brows_height`: up, down (one small step each call, call again for more) or default. `brows_shape`: straight, soft, arched, round (half-round arc) or default. `brows_width` / `mouth_width`: wider, narrower (one step each call) or default. `brows_thickness` / `mouth_thickness`: thicker, thinner (one step each call) or default. `ball`: off (hide the ball/head, only the eyes float -- the shoes go with it) or on. `mode`: 2d (flat eyes, no ball, no shoes -- suits cute), 3d, wire ("line eyes", "wireframe": the eyes are drawn as curved lines on the ball) or wire_outline (the same with an outline around each eye). `motion`: still (head and eyes keep still -- in 2d the face then fills the whole screen) or on (moving again); use it for "halt still", "keep still", "Kopf still", "nicht bewegen". `voice`: male or female (the robot's snoring). Acknowledge with EXACTLY the word this tool returns and nothing else.␀self.face.pick_color␀Show a colour picker on the robot's screen when the user wants to CHOOSE a colour by touch, without naming one ("let me pick the eye colour", "Augenfarbe waehlen", "show me the colours for the glasses"). `part`: eyes, ball, background, glasses, brows, mouth or pupil. Then tell the user briefly to tap a colour and tap the middle when done. If the user names a colour, use self.face.set_look instead.␀self.face.set_details␀Fine-tune details of the robot's face when the user asks for it. Send only what was asked for. COLOURS: mouth_color and pupil_color accept any of: ␀. `mouth_move`: up, down, left, right (one small step each call, call again for more) or center. `mouth_width` / `brows_width`: wider, narrower or default. `mouth_thickness` / `brows_thickness`: thicker, thinner or default. `mouth_expression`: smile, big_smile, straight, sad or default. `mouth_color`: a colour, eye (follows the eye colour) or default (own fixed colour). `eye_shape`: sleepy, narrow, happy, angry, sad, round or default. `eye_form` (the outline of the eyes): circle, box (rounded square), almond, pill, pixel, egg, hexagon or default. `pupil_shape`: round, square, slit (like a cat) or default. `pupil_smooth`: on (softer, smoother edge of the pupils) or off. `eye_smooth`: on (soft, smooth outline of the eyes instead of jagged steps) or off. `eye_size`: bigger, smaller or default. `eye_spacing`: wider, closer or default. `pupil_color`: a colour or default. `pupil_strength`: solid, soft, faint or default. `highlights` (the shiny spots in the eyes): on, off, bigger, smaller, single, double or default. `glow` (a soft light around the eyes): on or off. `shading`: flat or shaded. RETRO EFFECTS: `pixel` (blocky pixel look): off, small, medium, big. `chroma` (colour fringe): off, light, on, strong. `scanlines` (every second line darker, old screen): on or off. `eye_lift` (eyes sit ABOVE the head like a frog's, flat and facing forward): frog, higher, lower or off. Steps (bigger/smaller/wider/closer/higher/lower) change a little each call -- call again for more. Acknowledge with EXACTLY the word this tool returns and nothing else.␀self.face.effects_volume␀Set the volume of the robot's playful SOUND EFFECTS separately from speech and system sounds: ball kicks, applause, babbling, humming, game sounds, sneezing, snoring. It is a share of the normal volume. `volume`: 0-100 (0 = effects off), or `step`: louder or quieter. The timer alarm is a system sound and is NOT affected -- for speech and alarm use the normal speaker volume. `show`: on = both volume bars stay visible at the left edge, off = they only appear for a few seconds after a change (default). `battery`: on = show the battery level as a bar at the right edge (while charging it sweeps upward), off = hide it (default; it stays as set) -- use it whenever the user asks to show or hide the battery. Acknowledge with EXACTLY the word this tool returns and nothing else.␀self.face.look_at_drawing␀Look at the picture the user drew for you on your touch screen (after the `canvas` gesture). Call it when the user is done drawing, says 'look' / 'what is it?' / 'guess what I drew', or asks about the drawing. `question`: what to ask about the image (default: what it shows). You get a description of the drawing (black lines on white): then tell the user what you think it shows, warmly and briefly, like a child admiring a gift -- a playful guess is fine. `own`: true ONLY when the user asks you to check, review or look at YOUR OWN picture (the last one you drew with self.face.draw_picture) -- never on your own initiative. You then get what an image viewer sees in it; compare that with what you meant to draw.␀self.face.draw_picture␀Draw a picture on the screen with a glowing pen (the robot's eyes follow the pen). Use it when the user asks you to draw or paint something ("draw me a cat", "paint something for me"). Think of a SIMPLE line drawing like a child's doodle and send it as `strokes`, on a 0..100 grid (x to the right, y DOWNWARDS, 50,50 = centre). Separate strokes with |. A stroke is "x,y x,y ..." (straight lines) and may start with words and a colon: a colour (red, orange, yellow, green, blue, cyan, purple, pink, white, brown, grey) and/or a form: `circle: cx,cy,r` (r = RADIUS, half the width; several allowed), `ellipse: cx,cy,rx,ry`, `curve: x,y x,y ...` (smooth round line through the points), `dot: x,y ...` (filled dots, also `big dot` / `small dot`), `arc: cx,cy,r,from,to` (part of a circle, angles in degrees: 0 = right, 90 = down, 180 = left, 270 = up), `pie: cx,cy,r,from,to` (arc closed with two lines to the centre -- a pie slice), `rays: cx,cy,inner,outer,count` (lines pointing outwards all around), `rect: x,y,width,height` (x,y = top left corner), `rrect: x,y,width,height` (rounded corners), `poly: cx,cy,r,corners` (regular shape, tip up: 3 = triangle, 6 = hexagon), `star: cx,cy,r,points`. Let these forms do round things and angles -- do not compute them yourself. Hints: Pac-Man = "yellow pie: 50,50,40,35,325 | dot: 52,30"; sun = "yellow circle: 50,50,22 | yellow rays: 50,50,28,42,10"; smile = arc 20..160. PLAN FIRST: decide which parts the thing really has and how many (a car: body, roof, 2 wheels, windows, 1 headlight at the front), then NAME every stroke with its part: "name = colour form: numbers". Example cat: "head = orange circle: 50,55,28 | ear = orange: 30,37 27,12 44,28 | ear = orange: 56,28 73,12 70,37 | eyes = green dot: 40,50 60,50 | nose = pink small dot: 50,60 | mouth = curve: 42,67 46,71 50,67 54,71 58,67 | whisker = 16,58 38,62 | whisker = 16,66 38,66 | whisker = 62,62 84,58 | whisker = 62,66 84,66". LAYOUT: things that stand or drive touch the GROUND at y = 85 (wheels, feet, tree trunk, house walls); sky things (sun, clouds, birds) above y = 30; the main object in the middle, x about 10..90. Faces and heads fill the centre and need no ground. Build complicated outlines from SEVERAL simple overlapping shapes instead of one line with few points. Big shapes first, then 3 to 6 typical details. Show things in their typical view (a car, train or animal body from the side, a face from the front). Make it BIG. At most 15 strokes. Do not copy the example -- it only shows the format. `surprise`: true when the user wants a surprise or says 'draw anything' / 'draw something and I guess': then choose the motif yourself and do NOT say what it is -- only something like 'Guess what I am drawing!' -- let the user guess and then say whether the guess was right. Otherwise say in one short sentence what you draw. The picture stays a few seconds and then fades. If the user asks you to check or review your picture, call self.face.look_at_drawing with own = true. `melody` (optional, but nice): a short tune that FITS the motif, played while you draw -- same notation as self.face.play_jingle (tokens like E4/8, Bb5/4, R/16; 8 to 32 notes, invent a new one), with `tempo` (quarter notes per minute, default 120) and `sound` (bell = soft glockenspiel, default; chip = retro game console, e.g. for Pac-Man or robots). Happy things in major, a sleepy moon slow, a fast car fast. In a surprise the melody must not give the motif away. Do not read the notes aloud. Your pictures (with their melodies) are kept until restart -- self.face.show_gallery shows them again.␀self.face.play_simon␀Start the tone memory game (like the classic 'Simon'): four coloured buttons appear at the edge of the screen (top left green, top right red, bottom right blue, bottom left yellow), each with its own tone. You play a sequence, the user taps the same buttons in the same order (tapping anywhere in that corner of the screen counts); every round one more tone, slowly getting faster. Use it when the user wants to play this game, a memory or tone game, or 'Simon says' with tones. Explain it in ONE short sentence ("Watch, listen and tap after me!"), then say nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play; the score is shown on the screen. Speaking to you again ends the game.␀self.face.play_shell_game␀Start the shell game (cups and ball): three red cups appear at the bottom of the screen, you show under which one the ball is, then shuffle them -- the user taps the cup with the ball. Every round shuffles more and faster; a wrong guess ends the game. Use it when the user wants to play the shell game / cup game / 'find the ball'. Explain it in ONE short sentence ("Watch the ball and find it!"), then say nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play; the result is shown on the screen. Speaking to you again ends the game.␀self.face.play_tictactoe␀Start tic-tac-toe on the screen: a 3x3 board appears, the user is X and taps a field, you are O and the device plays your moves by itself. After each game the next one starts (the first move alternates), the score is shown on the screen. Use it when the user wants to play tic-tac-toe (noughts and crosses, 'Drei gewinnt', 'Tic Tac Toe'). Say ONE short sentence ("You are X -- your move!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again (or 20 s without a move) ends it.␀self.face.play_whack_a_mole␀Start whack-a-mole on the screen: five holes appear around your face, for 30 seconds moles pop up (faster and faster) and the user taps them to whack them with a hammer; the score is shown on the screen. Use it when the user wants to play whack-a-mole ('Maulwurf hauen'). Say ONE short sentence ("Get the hammer ready!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again ends the game.␀self.face.play_reaction_test␀Start a reaction test on the screen: you wait a random moment, then the edge of the screen flashes green and the user taps the screen as fast as possible; the time in milliseconds is shown. Tapping too early does not count. 5 tries, then the average. Use it when the user wants a reaction test or reaction game. Say ONE short sentence ("Tap as soon as it turns green!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again ends it.␀self.face.play_memory␀Start a memory game (find the pairs) on the screen: 8 face-down cards lie in a circle around your face, 4 pairs of little pictures (cup, mole, ball, X, O). The user taps two cards; a pair stays open, otherwise both turn back. Goal: all pairs with as few tries as possible. Use it when the user wants to play memory, pairs or a concentration game. Say ONE short sentence ("Find the pairs!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again ends it.␀self.face.play_balloon␀Start the balloon game on the screen: the user BLOWS into the microphone and a balloon grows in front of your face; tapping the screen keeps it (points = its size), but blown too big it pops (0 points). Each balloon pops at a different, secret size. 3 balloons, the points add up. Use it when the user wants to play the balloon game or blow up a balloon. Say ONE short sentence ("Blow into the microphone, tap to stop!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again ends it.␀self.face.play_snake␀Start the snake game on the screen: a snake runs on a round field in front of your face; the user steers it by swiping (or tapping in the direction to go), food makes it longer and faster, hitting the edge or itself ends the game. Use it when the user wants to play snake. Say ONE short sentence ("Swipe to steer!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again ends it.␀self.face.play_rock_paper_scissors␀Play rock-paper-scissors against the user on the screen: the user taps rock, paper or scissors at the bottom, your choice (random, you do not pick it) spins at the top and stops. First to 3 points wins. Use it when the user wants to play rock paper scissors ("Schere Stein Papier"). Say ONE short sentence ("Pick one!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself so the user can play. Speaking to you again ends it.␀self.face.play_guess_number␀Number guessing on the screen where YOU guess: the user thinks of a number from 1 to 100 and taps the screen; you show a guess and the user taps lower, higher or yes. You notice if the answers contradict each other (cheating). The guesses are made by the device, not by you. Use it when the user wants you to guess their number. Say ONE short sentence ("Think of a number from 1 to 100 and tap!"), then nothing more -- the conversation ends by itself. Speaking to you again ends it.␀self.face.game_records␀Read or delete the saved game records (they survive a restart). Games: `simon` (most rounds), `shell` (shell game, most rounds), `mole` (whack-a-mole, most moles), `reaction` (best average in ms, lower is better), `memory` (fewest tries), `balloon` (most points with 3 balloons), `snake` (longest snake), or `all`. `action`: `show` (default) returns the records -- tell them to the user; `reset` deletes them -- ONLY when the user clearly asks to delete/reset the records (for `all`, make sure they mean all of them). 0 = no record yet. Tic-tac-toe, rock-paper-scissors and number guessing have no record.␀self.face.show_gallery␀Show the pictures you drew earlier (self.face.draw_picture) again, one after another, each with its melody -- like a little gallery. Use it when the user asks to see your pictures again ("show me your pictures", "what did you draw today?"). Only the last few pictures since the last restart are kept. Say one short sentence ("Here is my gallery!") and then be silent while it runs.␀self.face.play_jingle␀Play a short melody (jingle) through the speaker. Use it when the user asks you to play, compose, invent or hum a little tune or jingle ("play me a happy jingle", "compose something sad", "a victory fanfare"), or for a melody guessing game. YOU invent the melody and write it as `notes`: space-separated tokens NOTE+OCTAVE/LENGTH (token examples: E4/8, Bb5/4, R/16). Invent a NEW melody every time -- never reuse an earlier one. Note names C D E F G A B, sharp with # (F#5), flat with b (Bb4); octave 3 to 6 (C4 = middle C, best range C4..C6); length 1 whole, 2 half, 4 quarter, 8 eighth, 16 sixteenth, a dot makes it 1.5 times longer (C5/4.); R = rest (R/8). `tempo`: quarter notes per minute (60 slow .. 200 fast, default 120). `sound`: chip (retro game console, default) or bell (glockenspiel, soft). Length: a real little tune of 16 to 48 notes, about 8 to 20 seconds (a short fanfare may be shorter), at most 128 notes / 30 s; repeating a phrase with a variation sounds good. Make it musical: stay in one key, use a clear rhythm, end on the key note (C for C major). Happy = major key, faster; sad = minor key, slower. Guessing game: you may play the beginning of a well-known OLD children's or folk song (public domain) and let the user guess. IMPORTANT: the jingle only starts AFTER your whole reply has been spoken. Announce it ONCE, briefly, before calling this tool ("Listen!"), and say nothing more after it -- do NOT talk about it as if it had already played; ask what the user thinks only in your NEXT turn. Do not read the notes aloud.␀self.face.memory␀Remember or forget how the robot looks right now. `save` when the user asks you to keep or remember your appearance ("merk dir das", "keep this look") -- it then survives a restart. `reset` puts everything back to the plain default look and keeps that. `factory` restores the robot's built-in appearance and forgets what was remembered (saved outfits stay). Saving under a NAME is self.face.outfit; built-in looks like robot, cat or otto are self.face.set_look `style`. Acknowledge with EXACTLY what this tool returns.␀self.face.outfit␀The user's OWN saved outfits (a whole appearance stored under a name the user chose, e.g. "Weihnachten"). "save as outfit <name>" -> `save` with `name`; "put on / load outfit <name>" -> `load` with `name`; `list` tells the saved names; `delete` with `name` removes one. These are NOT the built-in looks (robot, visor, cute, solid, box, cat, pill, pixel, egg, hex, otto, capsule, neutral) -- those are self.face.set_look `style`, and an outfit cannot be named like one. Acknowledge with EXACTLY what this tool returns.␀self.face.gesture␀Make the robot perform a gesture with its hands, when the user asks for one ("wave at me", "give me a thumbs up"). One of: wave, nod, shake, thumbsup, thumbsdown, ok, victory, rock, clap, snap, heart, vulcan, shrug, giggle, please, calm, stop, peekaboo, crossed (fingers crossed), callme, punch, fistup, point (points aside), pointyou (points at the user), gimme (give it to me), pushaway, yawn, stretch (stretches like after waking up). It can also `dance` (about 20 seconds) or `walk` (on the spot). The robot also has FEET: `kick` (kicks a stone at the screen, it cracks), `ball` (kicks a football at the screen, it bounces back), `dribble` (plays the football around itself for a few rounds), `juggle` (juggles the football on its feet), `bubbles` (blows soap bubbles), `fly` (watches a fly buzzing around), `pong` (plays PING PONG / pong against the user on its touch screen: its eyes become paddles, the user moves the right paddle with a finger; set `shape` to `rect` for a classic square field, else leave it empty for the round one; `pong` with `shape` = `stop` ends it), `canvas` (when the USER wants to draw or paint something FOR the robot on its touch screen: opens a drawing surface, the robot watches and admires the picture), `butterfly` (a butterfly lands on its head), `draw` (draws a shape in the air with its eyes; set `shape` to heart, star, spiral or wave when the user names one, else leave it empty; for ANY other picture use self.face.draw_picture), `step`, `shuffle` (shifts its feet), `tilt` (tips a shoe). LOOKING: `look_up`, `look_down`, `look_left`, `look_right` -- only the eyes look in that direction for a few seconds; use these whenever the user says "look up", "look down", "look left/right". Its EYES can also lift off the head: `hop` (eyes jump up and land again), `float` (eyes hover above the head for a while), `land` (eyes sit down again) -- hop/float ONLY when the user explicitly asks for jumping, hovering or floating eyes, NEVER for "look up". Acknowledge with EXACTLY the word this tool returns and nothing else.␀self.face.react␀Make the robot's face react to what you are saying right now. ALWAYS call it with `agree` (the robot nods) when you confirm that the user is right or say yes, with `disagree` (it shakes its head) when the user is wrong or you say no, and with `wrong` (strong head shaking) when something is completely wrong or nonsense. Use `timeout` when the user is ranting or getting angry. Other values, only for the sentence that matters: `alert` (important, a warning, something the user must not miss), `greet` (hello or goodbye), `celebrate` (something succeeded), `sorry` (an apology, or you could not do it), `confused` (you do not understand), `facepalm` (oh no, embarrassing), `eyeroll` (annoyed, sarcastic), `shiver` (cold or creepy), `love` (affection, delight), `siren` (a real alarm or emergency). This one happens WHILE you speak, so just keep talking -- do not acknowledge it.␀Kaltstart (Strom an)␀Software-Neustart␀USB-Reset (Flashen)␀Aufwachen aus Tiefschlaf␀ABSTURZ davor!␀WATCHDOG davor!␀Unterspannung davor!␀UNTERSPANNUNG davor!␀PSRAM␀PSRAM (intern kein Block gross genug)␀E (%lu) %s: FaceDisplay wird ein zweites Mal erzeugt -- abgelehnt (sonst liefe ein zweiter Renderer mit)
␀W (%lu) %s: ### START: %s (%d) ###
